---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { componentCode } from '../data/framework';
const repo = 'https://github.com/xandreeddev/desktop-lab/blob/main/';
const stages = [
  { id: 'update', title: 'Update state', owner: 'Desktop client · Rust', detail: 'An input event becomes a typed Message. Component::update changes the authoritative state. Opening the launcher retargets its width and height motions and marks the dock surface dirty. Async effects return their results as new messages.', code: 'Message → update(&mut self, message, effects)', state: 'launcher = true · target width = 448 logical px', file: 'lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/desktop.rs' },
  { id: 'view', title: 'Describe the view', owner: 'Desktop client + framework API', detail: 'When the dirty surface is ready to draw, the runtime builds ViewContext with the current monotonic time. view(&self, cx) samples the motions and returns an Element tree. This is a description; it does not talk to Vulkan or launch applications.', code: 'let width = self.panel_width.value(cx.now);', state: 'Elapsed time → eased progress → current width', file: 'lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/views.rs' },
  { id: 'layout', title: 'Resolve geometry', owner: 'lucent-ui · CPU', detail: 'Layout resolves logical rectangles from fixed/fill/shrink constraints, padding, gaps and alignment. It also produces hit targets and input regions. A component’s retarget_panel_transition helper only changes animation targets; the layout engine computes the actual rectangles.', code: 'Element tree → Scene { paint, hits, regions }', state: 'Logical rectangles, clip regions and interactive targets', file: 'lucent/crates/lucent-ui/src/lib.rs' },
  { id: 'paint', title: 'Build GPU work', owner: 'lucent-render · CPU + wgpu', detail: 'Paint is our display-list enum: Shape, Outline, Text and Image. The renderer obtains cached textures, uploads primitive records and viewport data, and records a render pass. Text coverage and mip levels are computed when a texture is first needed.', code: 'queue.write_buffer(…) → render_pass.draw(…)', state: 'Cached textures + primitive instance records', file: 'lucent/crates/lucent-render/src/lib.rs' },
  { id: 'present', title: 'Submit & compose', owner: 'wgpu → Vulkan → Hyprland', detail: 'wgpu submits commands to Vulkan and presents the surface buffer. Our WGSL shader draws the primitives. Hyprland combines this buffer with windows and other layer surfaces. The GPU rasterizes this frame; it does not own our 500 ms animation timeline.', code: 'queue.submit(…) → queue.present(frame)', state: 'Physical output pixels → compositor composition', file: 'lucent/crates/lucent-render/src/present.wgsl' },
  { id: 'schedule', title: 'Schedule or rest', owner: 'Wayland runtime + compositor', detail: 'A wl_surface.frame request is a one-shot callback associated with a commit. The callback clears the pending-frame gate. If motion remains active or another event made the surface dirty, the runtime draws again. Otherwise it waits. It does not repaint continuously while idle.', code: 'dirty && !pending → draw; otherwise wait', state: 'A callback is pacing information, not proof of display presentation', file: 'lucent/crates/lucent-wayland/src/lib.rs' },
];
const api = [
 ['Component', 'Own state, implement view and update; compose children with Element::map and Effects::delegate.'],
 ['Element<Message>', 'A declarative tree of rows, columns, stacks, grids, text, images, inputs and click/drag handlers.'],
 ['Effects<Message>', 'Request targeted redraws and run work on the effect worker. Results return as typed messages.'],
 ['Subscription<Message>', 'An identified stream of events with cancellation, used for clocks and external service updates.'],
 ['SurfaceSpec', 'Declare a native surface’s layer, anchors, dimensions, exclusive zone and keyboard behavior.'],
 ['Motion', 'A time-based scalar transition with retargeting, a duration and a cubic Bézier easing curve.'],
 ['WidgetRegistry', 'Register named widget views and descriptors, then compose them without changing the framework.'],
 ['Theme', 'The optional lucent-design crate supplies semantic roles, generated tokens and component recipes.'],
];
---
<Base title="Lucent documentation — state, components & rendering" description="Understand the Rust programming model, frame lifecycle, design system and Vulkan rendering pipeline behind Lucent.">
  <section class="guide-intro wrap"><a class="text-link" href="/">← Desktop Lab</a><span class="eyebrow">DOCUMENTATION / BENEATH THE SURFACE</span><h1>A desktop engine,<br /><span class="mint-text">you can follow.</span></h1><p>Start with the programming model. Follow one frame from state to pixels. Then build your own client on the same framework.</p></section>
  <div class="guide-layout wrap"><aside class="guide-nav"><span class="eyebrow">DEVELOPER GUIDE</span><nav aria-label="Documentation sections"><a href="#model">01 · Programming model</a><a href="#frame">02 · A frame, step by step</a><a href="#api">03 · Framework concepts</a><a href="#services">04 · Domain & use cases</a><a href="#rendering">05 · Rendering & density</a><a href="#quality">06 · Component quality</a><a href="#build">07 · Build & extend</a></nav></aside><div class="guide-content">
    <section id="model"><span class="eyebrow">01 / STRUCTS + TRAITS + FUNCTIONS</span><h2>Declarative views.<br />Explicit state changes.</h2><p>Lucent uses a hybrid Rust style: structs own state, traits define contracts, and functions compose data. There is no class inheritance. A component’s <code>view(&self, cx)</code> reads state and returns elements; <code>update(&mut self, message, effects)</code> handles a typed message and changes state.</p><p>Views stay free of OS operations. Use cases make decisions; service adapters do the external work. Effects deliver results back to the UI thread as messages. Rust’s ownership rules make those boundaries explicit.</p><CodeBlock code={componentCode} lang="rust" label="The core component contract" /><p>This minimal example illustrates the framework contract. For Lucent’s actual appearance, compose with the <a href="/design-system/#components">design-system recipes</a>. The launcher currently shares the desktop’s state struct; it has not yet been extracted into an independent launcher component.</p><div class="callout"><strong>If you know the browser engine</strong><p>Think of <code>Element</code> as a declarative view tree, <code>Layout</code> as layout and hit testing, <code>Paint</code> as a display list, and our renderer as the code that turns it into GPU work. This implementation rebuilds the affected surface’s tree and scene; it has no DOM diff or general CSS engine.</p></div></section>
    <section id="frame"><span class="eyebrow">02 / FOLLOW ONE FRAME</span><h2>When does each part run?</h2><p>Click a stage to follow opening the launcher. The first dirty frame can draw immediately when no frame is pending. Subsequent animated frames are paced by compositor callbacks.</p><div class="frame-walkthrough" data-tabs><div class="frame-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Frame lifecycle stages">{stages.map((stage,i) => <button role="tab" type="button" id={`stage-${stage.id}`} aria-controls={`frame-${stage.id}`} aria-selected={i===0 ? 'true' : 'false'} tabindex={i===0 ? 0 : -1}><span>0{i+1}</span>{stage.title}</button>)}</div>{stages.map((stage,i) => <div class="frame-detail" role="tabpanel" id={`frame-${stage.id}`} aria-labelledby={`stage-${stage.id}`} hidden={i!==0} tabindex="0"><span class="eyebrow">{stage.owner}</span><h3>{stage.title}</h3><p>{stage.detail}</p><code>{stage.code}</code><div class="frame-state">{stage.state}</div><a class="text-link" href={`${repo}${stage.file}`}>Read the implementation ↗</a></div>)}</div><p><strong>Who interpolates?</strong> Our CPU-side <code>Motion</code> implementation evaluates elapsed time and easing. Vulkan renders the values it receives. Wayland supplies surface protocols and frame pacing. An ordinary layer surface is not a secure lock screen.</p></section>
    <section id="api"><span class="eyebrow">03 / A SMALL VOCABULARY</span><h2>Compose behavior.<br />Keep the boundaries.</h2><dl class="guide-definitions">{api.map(([name,detail]) => <div><dt><code>{name}</code></dt><dd>{detail}</dd></div>)}</dl><p>The API contains no raw Wayland objects or GPU handles. Surface lifecycle belongs to <code>lucent-wayland</code>; GPU resource ownership belongs to <code>lucent-render</code>. <a href={`${repo}lucent/crates/lucent-api/src/lib.rs`}>Browse the complete API source ↗</a></p></section>
    <section id="services"><span class="eyebrow">04 / MEANING BEFORE MECHANICS</span><h2>The domain describes<br />what the desktop does.</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Responsibility</th><th>Concrete example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Domain</td><td>Models and service ports</td><td>Application, Workspace, Placement, Settings</td></tr><tr><td>Use cases</td><td>Decisions independent of rendering</td><td>Rank a search; launch or focus; clamp a drag</td></tr><tr><td>Adapters</td><td>External capabilities</td><td>XDG discovery, Hyprland IPC, system commands</td></tr><tr><td>Client</td><td>Map events to behavior and views</td><td>Open a result, display a failure, redraw the dock</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For example, clicking a dock icon prefers focusing a running application; selecting a launcher result starts a new instance. The use case chooses the behavior through ports. The XDG adapter launches an argument array and handles desktop-entry field codes without sending arbitrary commands through a shell.</p><p>Current system adapters include command-backed integrations. A complete typed D-Bus control center, notification history, tray, clipboard and IME remain future work. See the <a href="/guide/#ownership">ownership and scope guide</a> for the current split with Omarchy.</p></section>
    <section id="rendering"><span class="eyebrow">05 / FROM PAINT TO PIXELS</span><h2>Our renderer.<br />Established GPU machinery.</h2><p>We own the display-list translation, primitive pipeline, rounded-rectangle shader, texture cache and frame scheduling. wgpu supplies GPU access through Vulkan. fontdue supplies glyph coverage. SCTK supplies Wayland client building blocks.</p><ol class="guide-steps"><li>Layout and hit testing stay in logical pixels.</li><li>The buffer size is the logical surface size multiplied by its integer output scale.</li><li>Text uses shared kerning-aware advances. Glyph coverage is rasterized at 2× physical density and area-resolved to the output texture. Placement is snapped to physical pixels.</li><li>Image assets prefer SVG or larger originals. Previews use Lanczos filtering, then cached premultiplied mip levels smooth reductions as cards animate.</li><li>Primitive records go through <code>queue.write_buffer</code>, render-pass draws, <code>queue.submit</code> and <code>queue.present</code>.</li></ol><p>There is one shared GPU device for the application. Animated shapes retain subpixel coordinates, while settled text aligns to the pixel grid. The full framebuffer is not supersampled. Textures are cached, and the runtime stops requesting redraws when no work remains.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Current limits</strong><p>The prepared VM uses Mesa software Vulkan. Better texture quality does not turn that into hardware acceleration. Integer HiDPI is supported; fractional scale handling and complex-script shaping are still open. Historical performance numbers on the landing page describe the earlier 2-vCPU, 3-GiB baseline.</p></div></section>
    <section id="quality"><span class="eyebrow">COMPONENT QUALITY</span><h2>Test the pixels.<br />Test the interaction.</h2><p>The framework tracks hover, keyboard focus and controlled selection separately. <code>selected(true)</code> marks a selected row; <code>focus_within()</code> outlines a composite input. Focus color, width, caret and switch geometry come from the design system.</p><p>In the launcher, Tab and Shift+Tab cycle sections. Arrows select and Enter activates. Outside that composite, framework buttons use Tab traversal and Enter or Space activation.</p><p>Forty-eight native reference images cover light/dark, 1×/2×, scrolling, narrow widths, empty results, long input and a fixed animation frame. These run through the same Vulkan Canvas as the desktop. Geometry checks independently require every visible row to fit its clip and the caret to align with the input.</p><CodeBlock code="python3 vm/test-visual.py\npython3 vm/test-launcher.py" label="Host · native pixels and real keyboard input" /><p>Open <code>reports/local/visual-tests/index.html</code> to compare actual renders and highlighted differences. CI retains this report as an artifact. Intentional baseline changes require image review. See <a href="/design-system/#native-checks">native component captures</a>.</p></section><section id="build"><span class="eyebrow">07 / START SMALL</span><h2>Your first native surface.</h2><p>Use the independent <code>hello-layer</code> example to learn the component contract before extending the desktop. It runs inside a Wayland session with layer-shell support and a working Vulkan driver.</p><CodeBlock code="cargo run --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml -p hello-layer\n\ncargo test --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --workspace\ncargo doc --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --workspace --no-deps" label="Run, test and generate API documentation" /><p>To change the visual language, edit <code>design/tokens.json</code> and regenerate the consumers. To add a widget, implement a view and register its descriptor in the desktop widget registry. To add a system capability, start with its domain model, port and use case.</p><ul class="resource-list"><li><a href="/guide/">VM testing & installation ↗</a><span>Launch the prepared VM, use the shortcuts, install and roll back.</span></li><li><a href="/design-system/">The design system ↗</a><span>Semantic color, component recipes, motion and the complete token catalog.</span></li><li><a href={`${repo}docs/lucent-framework.md`}>Architecture in the repository ↗</a><span>Implementation boundaries, validation and current limitations.</span></li></ul></section>
  </div></div>
</Base>
